{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>How goodly are your tents, O Jacob.</b> It was stated in Chelak: Rabbi Elazar said, from the blessings of that wicked man you learn what was in his heart. He intended to say — may they not have synagogues and study halls; he said, ״מה טובו אהליך יעקב״. That the Shechinah not rest upon them; he said, ״משכנתיך ישראל״. That their kingdom not endure; he said, ״כנחלים נטיו״. That they not have olives and vineyards; he said, ״כגנות עלי נהר״. That their fragrance not waft; he said, ״כאהלים נטע ה׳״. That they not have tall kings; he said, ״כארזים עלי מים״. That they not have a king who is a son of a king; he said, ״יזל מים מדליו״. That their kingdom not rule over the nations; he said, ״וזרעו במים רבים״. That their kingdom not be mighty; he said, ״וגבה מאגג מלכו״. That their kingdom not be established; he said, ״ותנשא מלכותו״. Rabbi Abba bar Kahana said: all of them reverted to curse, except for synagogues and study halls, as it is said: ״ויהפוך ה׳ אלהיך לך את הקללה לברכה״ — curse, and not curses. Another interpretation: ״כנחלים נטיו״ — (the word נטיו) the language of נטיה applies to streams, as it is said: ״הנני נוטה אליה כנהר שלום״. Just as streams bend and spread this way and that when they swell, so Israel will be fruitful and multiply and need to spread out their tents and widen them, as He said: ״הרחיבי מקום אהלך״ etc." "<b>Like aloes planted by HaShem.</b> Like those precious plantings that HaShem planted in the Garden of Eden, as it is said: ״ויטע ה׳ אלהים גן בעדן מקדם״ — which grow and are blessed."], :book :numbers, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The text reproduces the passage from Sanhedrin 105b with many unvocalized and biblical forms; verse phrases left in Hebrew as they stand. וירום in the manuscript likely reflects וגבה of the verse; translated as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:10:11.107343111Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}